{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination-by-association",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "discrimination by association"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "Discrimination that occurs when an individual is treated less favourably, harassed, or otherwise disadvantaged because of their association with a person possessing a protected characteristic, such as race, religion, disability, sex, age, or sexual orientation."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "The key feature is that the discriminatory conduct is motivated by bias against the protected group but directed at someone linked or related to that group. This concept first achieved explicit legal recognition in European Union law through the landmark judgment of the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) in Coleman v. Attridge Law and Another (Case C‑303/06, 2008). The Court held that discrimination under Directive 2000/78/EC (the Employment Equality Framework Directive) is not limited to those who personally possess a protected characteristic. It also applies to situations where a person suffers adverse treatment because of their relationship with someone who does, such as a parent caring for a disabled child. The legal reasoning behind discrimination by association is grounded in the recognition that discriminatory motives operate relationally."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "6.3.2"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "discrimination"
    }
  },
